var e={notice:`The data here is/may be copyrighted and NOT included in the GPLv3 license. Converted from MegaMek's unit files (https://github.com/MegaMek/megamek): MegaMek Data (C) 2025-2026 by The MegaMek Team, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).`,shared:[{manufacturer:`Swedenborg Heavy Industries`,primaryFactory:`Kirchbach`,communications:`Swedenborg Heavy Industries Build 1685 Tacticom with ECM`,targeting:`Swedenborg Heavy Industries Series VI Integrated TTS`},{engine:`Swedenborg Heavy Industries 180`,armor:`Swedenborg Heavy Industries Magnum Ferro-Fibrous Composite`},`Cut off from their Homeworld production networks in the aftermath of the Word of Blake Jihad, Clan Hell's Horses faced a growing shortfall in infantry support vehicles. The designs that had long served the Clan's conventional forces could no longer be manufactured or restocked in the Inner Sphere. Rather than accept the continued decline of their vehicle arm, the Horses directed Swedenborg Heavy Industries on Kirchbach to develop an entirely new domestic platform. The result, introduced in 3084, was the Zephyros Infantry Support Vehicle. Taking its name from the Greek god of the West Wind, who in the form of a horse was said to draw the chariot of Zeus, the design embodied the Horses' philosophy: rapid response, battlefield endurance, and a focused mission over the distraction of multi-role versatility.`,`The Zephyros pairs a Fusion 180 powerplant with a dune-buggy style wheeled chassis and oversized wheels, reaching a cruising speed of 86 kilometers per hour and a top speed of 129. The rugged wheel arrangement gives the vehicle greater ground clearance than hover designs, improving durability in broken terrain. The trade-off is reduced all-terrain flexibility compared to tracked alternatives and less raw speed than hover platforms.

Survivability is a core design priority. Over a fifth of the vehicle's mass is dedicated to ferro-fibrous composite armor, and an integrated ECM suite helps defeat missile guidance systems and counter advanced targeting technology. Together, these features allow the crew to absorb fire from most light and medium threats and disengage before heavier units can close the range.

Primary armament is a pair of Series 2b extended-range medium lasers in the main turret, capable of engaging both Clan and Inner Sphere light armor at range. Series HL-II light machine guns provide the means to suppress or eliminate conventional infantry, the design's primary target category. The Zephyros is not intended as a standalone combatant. Its role is to protect friendly infantry on a contested battlefield, and commanders who push it into engagements against heavier armor will find its firepower wanting quickly.`,`The Zephyros emerged from a strategic problem that became acute in the years following the Word of Blake Jihad. Clan Hell's Horses had fought through three decades of conflict that slowly drained their conventional vehicle reserves, and with their ties to the Homeworld supply chains severed, replacement vehicles for aging designs such as the Odin, Mithras, and Indra were no longer forthcoming. The Horses' touman had been leaning harder on their remaining conventional units to compensate for BattleMech losses, and the gap between demand and available stock was widening. The Horses responded by commissioning an entirely new design from Swedenborg Heavy Industries, directing the facility's expanded manufacturing lines to produce what would become the Zephyros. Field trials were completed by the early 3080s, with full production beginning in 3084.

The new vehicle saw its first significant operational test on Toland in 3085, when intelligence confirmed that a pirate band known as Gort's Hounds was advancing on the planet to exploit its rare mineral deposits. A point of Zephyros vehicles was dispatched alongside Iota Galaxy's Sixty-Seventh BattleMech Cluster to support the cluster's Solhama infantry already stationed there. Working with Anhur VTOLs providing reconnaissance and acting as herders, the Zephyros crews laid an ambush for the approaching pirate column. The pirates fielded a mixed force of conventional vehicles and a handful of BattleMechs, and when the engagement opened, the Zephyros crews drove repeatedly into the enemy formation. Their heavy armor absorbed sustained fire while their extended-range lasers brought down one of the pirate BattleMechs and crippled a second. By the engagement's end, half of the Zephyros force had been destroyed or rendered barely operational, but the pirate column had been broken entirely.

The Toland action validated the design concept and encouraged Iota Galaxy commanders to formalize combined binarys pairing Anhur assault transports with Zephyros vehicles, creating self-contained infantry support packages capable of operating without heavier assets.`,`Zephyros Infantry Support Vehicle`,[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`clan-ecm-system`,`#0`,null,null],`ammo-clan-light-machine-gun-standard`,{manufacturer:`Aldis Industries`,primaryFactory:`Terra`,communications:`Olmstead Olmstead 30`,targeting:`Cirxese BallistaCheck / RockeCheck`},{engine:`225 ICE`,armor:`Aldis Industries ArcShield Max I`},`Aldis Industries introduced the Zhukov in 3030 as a deliberate follow-up to their venerable Demolisher, targeting customers who wanted the design philosophy of the 'Mech Slayer in a lighter, better-armored, and more mobile package. At 75 tons, the Zhukov trades raw destructive power for improved protection and a reduced target profile, qualities that Wolf's Dragoons identified immediately when they became the first major unit to deploy the type in quantity. The Dragoons' sustained operational feedback over the following decades drove a series of refinements and design improvements that Aldis incorporated into successive production runs, making the Zhukov one of the most field-tested combat vehicles of its era. Though smaller and less destructive than the Demolisher, the Zhukov's improved range and secondary weaponry give it a distinct tactical identity, filling roles the 'Mech Slayer cannot occupy effectively.`,`The Zhukov replaces the Demolisher's massive autocannons with twin SarLon MaxiCannon Autocannon/10s, sacrificing per-shot impact in exchange for improved range, ammunition efficiency, and the ability to engage targets at distances where the Demolisher cannot respond. A Harvester 20K SRM 6 launcher provides a secondary punch at close quarters, giving crews an option when engagement geometry favors missiles over cannon fire. The autocannons use an autoloader system derived directly from the Demolisher's ChemJet Gun autoloaders, simplifying crew training and cross-stocking for units operating both designs simultaneously.

ArcShield Max I armor provides better protection than the Demolisher's standard plate despite the Zhukov's lighter tonnage, reflecting Aldis's deliberate trade of offensive power for defensive durability. The smaller hull presents a reduced target profile that partially compensates for the more modest armor load. Running gear, suspension, and transmission are direct transfers from the Demolisher, while the turret geometry and fire control systems differ between the two designs. This parts commonality proved a significant logistical advantage in units operating mixed armored companies, reducing spare parts demands and simplifying field maintenance considerably.`,{front:44,left:32,right:32,rear:24,turret:44,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`autocannon-lbx-10`,`#4`,`turret`,null],`ammo-is-lb-10x-standard`,`Cirxese BallistaCheck / RockeCheck`,{manufacturer:`StarCorps Industries|StarCorps Industries|StarCorps Industries`,primaryFactory:`Crofton|Loburg|Terra`,communications:`O/P 3200 COMSET 4`,targeting:`O/P 2570`},{engine:`Strand 165 Fusion`,armor:`Durallex Special Heavy Ferro-Fibrous`},`Few combat hovertanks exemplify the model of lean, aggressive combined-arms warfare as well as the Zibler Fast Strike Tank. Introduced in 3096 by StarCorps Industries, the Zibler was designed from the outset as an OmniVehicle, giving field commanders maximum flexibility to configure it for whatever mission the moment demanded. Fast, adaptable, and available across the Inner Sphere, it proved itself one of the best combat vehicles the company ever brought to market. StarCorps wasted no time marketing it broadly, and within a generation the Zibler had found its way into the arsenals of nearly every major power. Its combat honors, spread across every realm, place it in some of the most climactic battles of the thirty-second century.`,`The Zibler keeps pace with almost any modern hovertank and offers enough armor and pod space for a varied and powerful weapons load. Its configurations are usually balanced for either close-in or ranged combat, though the D configuration is strictly a fire support hammer. The Strand 165 fusion engine drives the vehicle to a maximum speed of 129 kilometers per hour, placing the Zibler among the fastest medium combat vehicles available to Inner Sphere militaries and allowing it to dictate engagement range against most opponents.

Protection comes from seven tons of Durallex Special Heavy Ferro-Fibrous armor with an integrated CASE system, providing better staying power than lighter hover designs while keeping mass available for weapons. The eighteen tons of pod space give the Zibler its genuine strength: the OmniVehicle architecture allows configurations optimized for precision brawling, long-range harassment, anti-armor work, or command-network integration without retooling the base hull. Several configurations carry C3 networking equipment, letting a mixed lance of Ziblers share targeting data across range bands.

The primary limitation is the standard fusion engine, which consumes more tonnage than an extralight variant, capping the weapons payload compared to some contemporary designs. Crews also note that hover movement, while excellent on open ground, becomes a liability in dense urban terrain or broken ground.`,`StarCorps Industries introduced the Zibler Fast Strike Tank in 3096, naming it in honor of House Zibler, a Federated Suns noble family with a long tradition of military service. The company positioned the design as a universal hovertank that could serve any faction willing to purchase it. Within a decade, StarCorps was producing the Zibler at factories on Crofton, Loburg, and Terra, and the company openly stated its ambition to make the design as ubiquitous on the modern battlefield as the Vedette and Manticore had been during the Succession Wars.

The Zibler earned its first significant combat reputation during the Capellan Crusades, when units serving with the Republic Standing Guard demonstrated the value of mixing configurations against a single target. The design was prized as a cavalry unit, and its ability to engage at multiple range bands simultaneously attracted doctrine writers looking for flexible combined-arms platforms outside the BattleMech paradigm.

In 3112, during a raid on Acamar, a Capellan Confederation lance diverted toward a Republic staging area. RAF Lieutenant Stephen Belleau responded with two platoons of Ziblers from Firebase Howie, splitting them into three groups, each in a different configuration. Long-range autocannon units opened the engagement at distance. Heavy PPC units moved into the fight when the Capellan 'Mechs tried to press, punching through stealth armor that had already been exposed by the Ziblers' targeting computers. The close support group then closed to finish the engagement, and the Liao lance withdrew only after its commander's Cataphract was disabled. The engagement became a frequently cited example of multi-configuration hover tactics in subsequent doctrine manuals.

Belleau was decorated and promoted for the Acamar action, eventually rising to command a cavalry battalion that mixed fast hovertanks, BattleMechs, and battle armor. He became an outspoken critic of Devlin Stone's disarmament policies, arguing that the battles of the Crusades demonstrated what the Republic was giving up. When the HPG Blackout struck, Belleau was killed leading a scratch defense force protecting one of the relay stations targeted by ground attack. By then the Zibler had spread well beyond its Federated Suns origins and served in militaries across every major power in the Inner Sphere.`,[{tag:`quirk-fast-reload`}],`Zibler Fast Strike Tank`,{front:30,left:30,right:30,rear:20,turret:28,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`case`,`#0`,null,null,null],`ammo-is-light-ac-2-standard`,[`tag`,`uuid`,`pod`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`],`TRTTS Mark II with Artemis IV FCS`,{engine:`ICE 140`,armor:`Forging OTR17b`},`Named for a dawn goddess of ancient Slavic mythology, the Zorya Light Tank holds a unique distinction as the single most common armored combat vehicle in service with the Clans of Kerensky. Introduced in 2842 during the upheaval of the Succession Wars era, the design was conceived not as a frontline predator but as a practical, affordable solution to the Clans' need for garrison firepower across dozens of Homeworld facilities. Built from commonly available parts with an internal combustion engine and conventional armor plate, the Zorya is inexpensive to manufacture and demands little specialized training from its three-person crew. Its long-range weapons give it a useful fire-support role in garrison and second-line clusters throughout Clan space.`,`The Zorya is powered by an ICE powerplant, which limits its flanking speed to 65 km/h and places it at a disadvantage against most purpose-built military vehicles. The vehicle carries two weapons in its fully traversing turret, giving the crew 360-degree coverage. The Type OVR-X LB 5-X Autocannon cycles between armor-piercing slugs and cluster munitions, making it effective against infantry concentrations and light vehicles as well as heavier armor. A Type X "Short Bow" LRM-10 launcher with an integrated Artemis IV Fire Control System complements the autocannon with guided long-range missile fire. Both weapons can engage VTOLs and other aircraft with reasonable effectiveness. The Zorya carries forty rounds for the autocannon, split between ammunition types, providing substantial endurance in sustained engagements.

The vehicle's armor is concentrated toward the front hull and turret, but uses conventional plate rather than ferro-fibrous construction, leaving it approximately 20 percent less resistant to damage than a design using advanced composites. The integrated ECM suite supports friendly units nearby but tends to attract enemy fire, meaning the Zorya depends on maintaining range from heavy opponents. The commander doubles as the communications and electronic warfare officer, allowing the three-person crew to manage the vehicle's sensor and ECM systems efficiently, though this dual role leaves little redundancy when crew losses occur.`,[`clan-autocannon-lbx-5`,`#1`,`turret`,null],`ammo-clan-lb-5x-cluster`,`ammo-clan-lb-5x-standard`,{front:18,left:10,right:10,rear:10,turret:16,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`ammo-clan-lb-5x-cluster`,`#5`,null,`clan-autocannon-lbx-5`],[`ammo-clan-lb-5x-standard`,`#6`,null,`clan-autocannon-lbx-5`]],designs:JSON.parse(`{"1050":{"tro":{"production":"~0","components":"~1","text":{"overview":"~2","capabilities":"~3","deployment":"Rolling off production lines at the Swedenborg Heavy Industries complex on Kirchbach, the Zephyros entered widespread service across the Horses Touman from 3084. Its straightforward construction and modest resource demands allowed Swedenborg to build up delivery rates quickly. The vehicle proved especially popular with Iota Galaxy, whose commanders embraced the concept of pairing Zephyros units with Anhur assault transports, creating integrated support packages capable of delivering Elementals into contested ground and sustaining them without heavier assets. Front-line infantry commanders across multiple clusters expressed strong interest in the design, drawn to its combination of speed, armor, and dedicated anti-infantry armament.","battleHistory":"~4"}},"name":"~5","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","tech":"clan","era":"jihad","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":31,"left":18,"right":18,"rear":18,"turret":20,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~6","$v":["~7",["er-medium-laser-clan","#1","turret",null],["er-medium-laser-clan","#2","turret",null],["clan-light-machine-gun","#3","turret",null],["clan-light-machine-gun","#4","turret",null],["~8","#5",null,"clan-light-machine-gun"]]}},"1051":{"tro":{"production":"~0","components":"~1","text":{"overview":"~2","capabilities":"~3","deployment":"Introduced in 3086, the Dual Turret variant addressed the most common field complaint about the base model: the limited engagement arc of its light machine guns when the main turret was tracking an armored target. Moving the machine guns to a free-floating pintle mount at the rear of the vehicle allows the gunner to engage infantry independently of the laser turret, significantly improving simultaneous engagement capability. The cost is modest, a downgrade of the forward lasers to standard medium range and a reduction of half a ton of ammunition to accommodate the second turret mechanism. The variant was fielded selectively alongside base models in clusters where anti-infantry suppression was prioritized over longer-range engagements.","battleHistory":"~4"}},"name":"~5","model":"(Dual Turret)","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","dualTurret":true,"tech":"clan","era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":{"front":23,"left":18,"right":18,"rear":14,"turret":16,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":16},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","location","halfTon","feedsWeaponTag"],"$v":[["clan-ecm-system","#0",null,null,null],["clan-light-machine-gun","#1","turret",null,null],["clan-light-machine-gun","#2","turret",null,null],["er-medium-laser-clan","#3","turret2",null,null],["er-medium-laser-clan","#4","turret2",null,null],["~8","#5",null,true,"clan-light-machine-gun"]]}},"1052":{"tro":{"production":"~9","components":"~10","text":{"overview":"~11","capabilities":"~12","deployment":"The standard Zhukov introduced in 3030 and first deployed in strength by Wolf's Dragoons. The design subsequently spread to the Free Worlds League, Capellan Confederation, and numerous mercenary formations. Betelgeuse production under Capellan control ensured continued availability through the late Succession Wars and Clan Invasion eras. The Dragoons' sustained operational use made this one of the most field-tested armored vehicles of its generation.","battleHistory":"The Zhukov was conceived as Aldis Industries' answer to a market reality: the Demolisher's reputation had created demand for a vehicle with similar characteristics, but the original design's weight, cost, and limited secondary armament made it unsuitable for many of the roles customers actually needed to fill. Rather than license a competitor or develop an entirely new platform, Aldis engineers worked from the Demolisher's successful mechanical foundation, retaining the proven drivetrain, running gear, and autoloading systems while building a new hull and turret around a less extreme weapons philosophy.\\n\\nWolf's Dragoons adopted the Zhukov immediately on its 3030 introduction, deploying battalion-strength formations that provided the design's real-world testing program. The Dragoons' operational experience revealed a consistent pattern of strengths and weaknesses, and their feedback loop with Aldis became the primary driver of the production improvements that distinguished later Zhukov runs from early ones. Over twenty-eight years of manufacture between 3030 and 3058, the Dragoons' suggestions shaped the vehicle's fire control systems, ammunition stowage arrangements, and crew station ergonomics in ways the original specification had not anticipated.\\n\\nThe Zhukov's most famous engagement came during the War of Andurien Succession in 3031 on Watermael. A Marik commander deployed a Zhukov battalion against a Demolisher battalion that had broken through the front and was advancing on a Marik logistics base through sugar cane fields. The terrain proved decisive in unexpected ways. Tall cane blocked all gunnery sights, forcing crews on both sides to track targets by observing the movement of the stalks. The Zhukovs used the same limitation to their advantage, lighter weight allowed them to maneuver through marshy flanking ground the heavier Demolishers could not cross, and their longer-ranged autocannons gave them engagement windows the Demolishers could not match in the obscured terrain. By the time the Demolishers entered effective range, twenty-five of the battalion's tanks were burning. The remaining Demolishers were destroyed within minutes.\\n\\nThis engagement became a standard case study in combined arms training programs across the Inner Sphere, demonstrating that the Demolisher and Zhukov, though sharing a design heritage, filled genuinely different tactical roles. Where the Demolisher excelled at close-range ambush and prepared defensive positions, the Zhukov's range advantage made it more effective in fluid engagements where terrain and distance could be manipulated.\\n\\nAldis discontinued Zhukov development in 3058, concluding that the advancing technological environment required more investment than the existing platform could support. The Terra facility operated under ComStar's auspices by that point, and within a decade the Word of Blake had assumed operational control, developing their own variant to suit Blakist requirements during the Jihad. Post-Jihad reconstruction shifted the Terra line to a C³-networked configuration, adapting the aging platform to the Republic of the Sphere's combined arms doctrine. The Capellan-controlled Betelgeuse facility followed with their own C³ variant, ensuring the design remained available to Capellan forces through changing political landscapes.\\n\\nThe Zhukov's Dark Age story diverges along geographic lines. The Betelgeuse line continued producing the Liao variant through the ilClan period, while the Terra facility's output reflected the successive political transformations of the planet itself, from Word of Blake Protectorate through Republic stewardship and into the upheavals of the late Dark Age. The design that Wolf's Dragoons had first deployed in 3030 proved durable enough to remain relevant through the 32nd century, not because it had been dramatically updated but because its fundamental qualities (range, reliability, and parts commonality with the ubiquitous Demolisher) continued to offer value even in a technological environment that had long since left its original specifications behind."}},"name":"Zhukov Heavy Tank","tonnage":75,"era":"late-sw-rn","armorAllocation":"~13","equipment":{"$k":["tag","location","uuid","feedsWeaponTag"],"$v":[["srm-6","front","#0",null],["autocannon-standard-c","turret","#1",null],["autocannon-standard-c","turret","#2",null],["ammo-ac-10-standard",null,"#3","autocannon-standard-c"],["ammo-ac-10-standard",null,"#4","autocannon-standard-c"],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#5","srm-6"]]}},"1053":{"tro":{"production":"~9","components":"~10","text":{"overview":"~11","capabilities":"~12","deployment":"Produced at the Terra facility during the Jihad period, this variant upgrades the autocannons to LB-10X models and reduces the SRM launcher to a 4-tube configuration, freeing space for C3i computer installation and CASE protection. The combination of improved autocannons and targeting network integration substantially increases combat effectiveness over the standard model. A Capellan copy uses a standard C³ system with a Guardian ECM suite instead of C3i, optimizing the design for Capellan combined arms doctrine.","battleHistory":"The Zhukov was conceived as Aldis Industries' answer to a market reality: the Demolisher's reputation had created demand for a vehicle with similar characteristics, but the original design's weight, cost, and limited secondary armament made it unsuitable for many of the roles customers actually needed to fill. Rather than license a competitor or develop an entirely new platform, Aldis engineers worked from the Demolisher's successful mechanical foundation, retaining the proven drivetrain, running gear, and autoloading systems while building a new hull and turret around a less extreme weapons philosophy.\\n\\nWolf's Dragoons adopted the Zhukov immediately on its 3030 introduction, deploying battalion-strength formations that provided the design's real-world testing program. The Dragoons' operational experience revealed a consistent pattern of strengths and weaknesses, and their feedback loop with Aldis became the primary driver of the production improvements that distinguished later Zhukov runs from early ones. Over twenty-eight years of manufacture between 3030 and 3058, the Dragoons' suggestions shaped the vehicle's fire control systems, ammunition stowage arrangements, and crew station ergonomics in ways the original specification had not anticipated.\\n\\nThe Zhukov's most famous engagement came during the War of Andurien Succession in 3031 on Watermael. A Marik commander deployed a Zhukov battalion against a Demolisher battalion that had broken through the front and was advancing on a Marik logistics base through sugar cane fields. The terrain proved decisive in unexpected ways. Tall cane blocked all gunnery sights, forcing crews on both sides to track targets by observing the movement of the stalks. The Zhukovs used the same limitation to their advantage - lighter weight allowed them to maneuver through marshy flanking ground the heavier Demolishers could not cross, and their longer-ranged autocannons gave them engagement windows the Demolishers could not match in the obscured terrain. By the time the Demolishers entered effective range, twenty-five of the battalion's tanks were burning. The remaining Demolishers were destroyed within minutes.\\n\\nThis engagement became a standard case study in combined arms training programs across the Inner Sphere, demonstrating that the Demolisher and Zhukov, though sharing a design heritage, filled genuinely different tactical roles. Where the Demolisher excelled at close-range ambush and prepared defensive positions, the Zhukov's range advantage made it more effective in fluid engagements where terrain and distance could be manipulated.\\n\\nAldis discontinued Zhukov development in 3058, concluding that the advancing technological sphere required more investment than the existing platform could support. The Terra facility operated under ComStar's auspices by that point, and within a decade the Word of Blake had assumed operational control, developing their own variant to suit Blakist requirements during the Jihad. Post-Jihad reconstruction shifted the Terra line to a C³-networked configuration, adapting the aging platform to the Republic of the Sphere's combined arms doctrine. The Capellan-controlled Betelgeuse facility followed with their own C³ variant, ensuring the design remained available to Capellan forces through changing political landscapes.\\n\\nThe Zhukov's Dark Age story diverges along geographic lines. The Betelgeuse line continued producing the Liao variant through the ilClan period, while the Terra facility's output reflected the successive political transformations of the planet itself, from Word of Blake Protectorate through Republic stewardship and into the upheavals of the late Dark Age. The design that Wolf's Dragoons had first deployed in 3030 proved durable enough to remain relevant through the 32nd century, not because it had been dramatically updated but because its fundamental qualities (range, reliability, and parts commonality with the ubiquitous Demolisher) continued to offer value even in a technological environment that had long since left its original specifications behind."}},"name":"Zhukov Heavy Tank","model":"(LB-X)","tonnage":75,"era":"jihad","armorAllocation":"~13","equipment":{"$k":"~6","$v":[["c3i-computer","#0",null,null],["case","#1",null,null],["srm-4","#2","front",null],["autocannon-lbx-10","#3","turret",null],"~14",["~15","#5",null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster","#6",null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-srm-standard","#7",null,"srm-4"]]}},"1054":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Aldis Industries|Aldis Industries|Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Betelgeuse|Terra|Various","communications":"Olmstead Olmstead 30","targeting":"~16"},"components":"~10","text":{"overview":"~11","capabilities":"~12","deployment":"Post-Jihad production variant fitted with standard C³ networking capability, adapted for combined arms operations under Republic and later Capellan doctrine. Produced at both the Terra and Betelgeuse facilities and also available as a refit conversion of existing Zhukov hulls. The Capellan-controlled Betelgeuse line has continued producing this variant through the ilClan period, making it the most broadly distributed Zhukov configuration in the modern era.","battleHistory":"The Zhukov was conceived as Aldis Industries' answer to a market reality: the Demolisher's reputation had created demand for a vehicle with similar characteristics, but the original design's weight, cost, and limited secondary armament made it unsuitable for many of the roles customers actually needed to fill. Rather than license a competitor or develop an entirely new platform, Aldis engineers worked from the Demolisher's successful mechanical foundation, retaining the proven drivetrain, running gear, and autoloading systems while building a new hull and turret around a less extreme weapons philosophy.\\n\\nWolf's Dragoons adopted the Zhukov immediately on its 3030 introduction, deploying battalion-strength formations that provided the design's real-world testing program. The Dragoons' operational experience revealed a consistent pattern of strengths and weaknesses, and their feedback loop with Aldis became the primary driver of the production improvements that distinguished later Zhukov runs from early ones. Over twenty-eight years of manufacture between 3030 and 3058, the Dragoons' suggestions shaped the vehicle's fire control systems, ammunition stowage arrangements, and crew station ergonomics in ways the original specification had not anticipated.\\n\\nThe Zhukov's most famous engagement came during the War of Andurien Succession in 3031 on Watermael. A Marik commander deployed a Zhukov battalion against a Demolisher battalion that had broken through the front and was advancing on a Marik logistics base through sugar cane fields. The terrain proved decisive in unexpected ways. Tall cane blocked all gunnery sights, forcing crews on both sides to track targets by observing the movement of the stalks. The Zhukovs used the same limitation to their advantage, lighter weight allowed them to maneuver through marshy flanking ground the heavier Demolishers could not cross, and their longer-ranged autocannons gave them engagement windows the Demolishers could not match in the obscured terrain. By the time the Demolishers entered effective range, twenty-five of the battalion's tanks were burning. The remaining Demolishers were destroyed within minutes.\\n\\nThis engagement became a standard case study in combined arms training programs across the Inner Sphere, demonstrating that the Demolisher and Zhukov, though sharing a design heritage, filled genuinely different tactical roles. Where the Demolisher excelled at close-range ambush and prepared defensive positions, the Zhukov's range advantage made it more effective in fluid engagements where terrain and distance could be manipulated.\\n\\nAldis discontinued Zhukov development in 3058, concluding that the advancing technological theater required more investment than the existing platform could support. The Terra facility operated under ComStar's auspices by that point, and within a decade the Word of Blake had assumed operational control, developing their own variant to suit Blakist requirements during the Jihad. Post-Jihad reconstruction shifted the Terra line to a C³-networked configuration, adapting the aging platform to the Republic of the Sphere's combined arms doctrine. The Capellan-controlled Betelgeuse facility followed with their own C³ variant, ensuring the design remained available to Capellan forces through changing political landscapes.\\n\\nThe Zhukov's Dark Age story diverges along geographic lines. The Betelgeuse line continued producing the Liao variant through the ilClan period, while the Terra facility's output reflected the successive political transformations of the planet itself, from Word of Blake Protectorate through Republic stewardship and into the upheavals of the late Dark Age. The design that Wolf's Dragoons had first deployed in 3030 proved durable enough to remain relevant through the 32nd century, not because it had been dramatically updated but because its fundamental qualities (range, reliability, and parts commonality with the ubiquitous Demolisher) continued to offer value even in a technological environment that had long since left its original specifications behind."}},"name":"Zhukov Heavy Tank","model":"(Liao)","tonnage":75,"era":"jihad","armorAllocation":"~13","equipment":{"$k":"~6","$v":[["c3-computer-slave","#0",null,null],["ecm-suite","#1",null,null],["case","#2",null,null],["srm-4","#3","front",null],"~14",["autocannon-lbx-10","#5","turret",null],["~15","#6",null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster","#7",null,"autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-srm-standard","#8",null,"srm-4"]]}},"1055":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The A Configuration trades the Prime's close-range punch for sustained long-range harassment. Four Light Autocannon/2 weapons supply a broad spread of fire with ninety rounds of ammunition from a two-ton bay, permitting extended engagements without resupply. The configuration exploits the Zibler's speed to maintain stand-off distance against heavier opponents, wearing them down while faster or harder-hitting partners maneuver for decisive action. The A excels at suppression and flank harassment rather than direct engagement. Its light autocannons lack the penetrating power to seriously threaten well-armored targets alone, and the A functions best as a supporting element in a mixed-configuration lance rather than as a primary striker.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"quirks":"~22","name":"~23","model":"(A)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","omni":true,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~24","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","location","pod","feedsWeaponTag"],"$v":["~25",["autocannon-light-2","#1","turret",true,null],["autocannon-light-2","#2","turret",true,null],["autocannon-light-2","#3","turret",true,null],["autocannon-light-2","#4","turret",true,null],["~26","#5",null,null,"autocannon-light-2"],["~26","#6",null,null,"autocannon-light-2"]]}},"1056":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The B Configuration fills an intermediate role, combining an LB 10-X Autocannon with two extended-range medium lasers and a four-gun light machine gun array. The LB-X provides both anti-armor capability with standard rounds and area suppression with cluster munitions, backed by the extended-range lasers for consistent damage at mid-range. The machine gun array discourages infantry from approaching the vehicle. The B is the most versatile of the standard Zibler configurations, capable of contributing meaningfully at multiple range bands and against varied target types. Commanders use it as a general-purpose workhorse when mission parameters are uncertain or when the force must engage without knowing whether it will face armor, infantry, or both.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"quirks":"~22","name":"~23","model":"(B)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","omni":true,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~24","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","location","pod","feedsWeaponTag","halfTon"],"$v":[["case","#0",null,null,null,null],["er-medium-laser","#1","turret",true,null,null],["er-medium-laser","#2","turret",true,null,null],["autocannon-lbx-10","#3","turret",true,null,null],["machine-gun-light","#4","turret",true,null,null],["machine-gun-light","#5","turret",true,null,null],["machine-gun-light","#6","turret",true,null,null],["machine-gun-light","#7","turret",true,null,null],["is-light-machine-gun-array","#8","turret",true,null,null],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster","#9",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10",null],["ammo-is-light-machine-gun-standard","#10",null,null,"machine-gun-light",true],["~15","#11",null,null,"autocannon-lbx-10",null]]}},"1057":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The C Configuration offers mixed-range firepower for support across multiple engagement distances. Twin seven-tube Multi-Missile Launchers carry two tons of ammunition and can engage effectively at both long and short range depending on munition selection, while a Snub-Nose PPC delivers a solid punch at close range. A C3 slave unit matches those found in the Prime and D configurations, allowing the C to participate in coordinated targeting networks and draw on data shared by scout elements. The missile loadout makes the C effective against clustered infantry and light vehicles at range, and the Snub-Nose PPC provides a deterrent against anything that closes in. The C is most effective when networked with other C3-equipped lancemates.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"quirks":"~22","name":"~23","model":"(C)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","omni":true,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~24","equipment":{"$k":"~27","$v":["~25",["c3-computer-slave","#1",true,null,null],["snub-nose-ppc","#2",true,"turret",null],["mml-7","#3",true,"turret",null],["mml-7","#4",true,"turret",null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#5",null,null,"mml-7"],["ammo-srm-standard","#6",null,null,"mml-7"]]}},"1058":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The D Configuration is the heaviest-hitting Zibler variant and among the most capable single-weapon hovertank loadouts available at this weight class. A single Heavy Particle Projector Cannon dominates the turret, striking targets at range with enough force to threaten assault-class BattleMechs. A C3 boosted slave unit enhances the D's accuracy when operating in a networked lance, allowing targeting data from distant scouts to guide PPC fire. The D excels at long-range fire support and priority-target elimination, and commanders reserve D configurations for engagements where a single high-value unit must be removed quickly. The single-weapon layout is easy to manage under fire, but leaves little redundancy if the PPC sustains damage. As a pure hammer, it performs best when supported by configurations that can engage secondary targets independently.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"quirks":"~22","name":"~23","model":"(D)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","omni":true,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~24","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","pod","location"],"$v":[["c3-boosted-slave","#0",true,null],["case","#1",null,null],["heavy-ppc","#2",true,"turret"]]}},"1059":{"tro":{"production":"~17","components":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"The Primary Configuration is built for close-in combat. Four medium lasers and three four-tube Streak Short-Range Missile launchers are paired with a targeting computer in the turret, maximizing accuracy at short ranges and ensuring the Streak missiles only fire on confirmed locks. A C3 slave unit allows the Prime to share targeting data across a coordinated lance, and two additional heat sinks manage the thermal demands of the laser battery. The configuration performs best when the Zibler's speed advantage can be used to close range quickly, disrupt enemy formation, and force engagements on favorable terms. Commanders favor the Prime for aggressive pursuit, defensive suppression, and actions where enemy units must be disabled rather than destroyed outright.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"quirks":"~22","name":"~23","model":"(Prime)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","omni":true,"era":"early-rep","engineType":"ice","cruiseMP":8,"armorType":"heavy-ferro-fibrous","armorAllocation":"~24","equipment":{"$k":"~27","$v":[["c3-computer-slave","#0",true,null,null],["targeting-computer","#1",true,null,null],["case","#2",null,null,null],["medium-laser","#3",true,"turret",null],["medium-laser","#4",true,"turret",null],["medium-laser","#5",true,"turret",null],["medium-laser","#6",true,"turret",null],["streak-srm-4","#7",true,"turret",null],["streak-srm-4","#8",true,"turret",null],["streak-srm-4","#9",true,"turret",null],["ammo-is-streak-srm-standard","#10",null,null,"streak-srm-4"]]}},"1060":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Various","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6c","targeting":"~28"},"components":"~29","text":{"overview":"~30","capabilities":"~31","deployment":"The standard Zorya serves in garrison clusters and second-line formations across Clan space. Typically deployed in groups to provide overlapping fields of fire, the vehicle uses its LRM-10 and LB 5-X Autocannon to engage targets at standoff range while supporting infantry and heavier vehicles in combined-arms operations. The forty-round autocannon magazine, divided between cluster and slug munitions, gives crew commanders flexibility to engage various target types without resupply. Most Clans assign Zoryas to solahma units and lower-caste garrison forces, where the vehicle's straightforward controls allow non-warrior crews to operate it effectively with minimal training time.","battleHistory":"The Zorya emerged in 2842 from a recognition among Clan military planners that garrison operations required large numbers of affordable, easily crewed vehicles rather than small numbers of elite equipment. The name, drawn from ancient Slavic legend where the Zorya were dawn goddesses who guarded against cosmic destruction, was chosen to reflect the vehicle's protective garrison role rather than an aggressive frontline identity.\\n\\nProduction spread rapidly across multiple Homeworld facilities, and nearly every Clan integrated the Zorya into its second-line and garrison clusters. Clan Blood Spirit and Clan Cloud Cobra were among the earliest heavy adopters, fielding the vehicle in significant numbers through their lower-caste garrison forces. Blood Spirit Zoryas suffered severe losses during the Star Adder-Burrock Trial of Absorption, when Clan Blood Spirit intervened and found its garrison units badly outmatched. The losses proved difficult to replace quickly despite the vehicle's low unit cost.\\n\\nThe design gained renewed prominence following Operation Revival and the Refusal War. Clan Wolf, its warrior population significantly depleted by the Inner Sphere campaign and the fighting against Clan Jade Falcon, turned to the Zorya as an affordable platform for rebuilding garrison strength. Khan Vlad Ward authorized recruiting members of the artisan, laborer, and merchant castes through Trials of Position to crew Zoryas and other vehicles, acknowledging that conventional warrior recruitment could not fill the gap quickly enough.\\n\\nThe Zorya's longevity is evidence of sound, if unambitious, design. No single Clan has dominated its production, and the vehicle remains a fixture of Clan garrison forces well into the ilClan era."}},"name":"Zorya Light Tank","tonnage":35,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":15,"left":9,"right":9,"rear":9,"turret":14,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~6","$v":["~7","~32",["clan-lrm-10-artemis-iv","#2","turret",null],["ammo-clan-lrm-artemis-iv","#3",null,"clan-lrm-10-artemis-iv"],["~33","#4",null,"clan-autocannon-lbx-5"],["~34","#5",null,"clan-autocannon-lbx-5"]]}},"1061":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Various","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6c","targeting":"~28"},"components":"~29","text":{"overview":"~30","capabilities":"~31","deployment":"Introduced alongside the standard Zorya in 2842, the Ammo variant removes the Artemis IV Fire Control System from the LRM-10 launcher and replaces it with expanded autocannon ammunition storage. The additional magazine capacity allows crews to sustain fire longer without choosing between munition types, a practical advantage in extended garrison operations where resupply may be limited. Reinforced frontal and turret armor improves survivability in head-on exchanges at the cost of the guided-missile accuracy the Artemis system provides. The Ammo variant is common in second-line clusters where attrition sustainability matters more than per-shot hit probability.","battleHistory":"The Zorya emerged in 2842 from a recognition among Clan military planners that garrison operations required large numbers of affordable, easily crewed vehicles rather than small numbers of elite equipment. The name, drawn from ancient Slavic legend where the Zorya were dawn goddesses who guarded against cosmic destruction, was chosen to reflect the vehicle's protective garrison role rather than an aggressive frontline identity.\\n\\nProduction spread rapidly across multiple Homeworld facilities, and nearly every Clan integrated the Zorya into its second-line and garrison clusters. Clan Blood Spirit and Clan Cloud Cobra were among the earliest heavy adopters, fielding the vehicle in significant numbers through their lower-caste garrison forces. Blood Spirit Zoryas suffered severe losses during the Star Adder-Burrock Trial of Absorption, when Clan Blood Spirit intervened and found its garrison units badly outmatched. The losses proved difficult to replace quickly despite the vehicle's low unit cost.\\n\\nThe design gained renewed prominence following Operation Revival and the Refusal War. Clan Wolf, its warrior population significantly depleted by the Inner Sphere campaign and the fighting against Clan Jade Falcon, turned to the Zorya as an affordable platform for rebuilding garrison strength. Khan Vlad Ward authorized recruiting members of the artisan, laborer, and merchant castes through Trials of Position to crew Zoryas and other vehicles, acknowledging that conventional warrior recruitment could not fill the gap quickly enough.\\n\\nThe Zorya's longevity is a reflection of sound, if unambitious, design. No single Clan has dominated its production, and the vehicle remains a fixture of Clan garrison forces well into the ilClan era."}},"name":"Zorya Light Tank","model":"(Ammo)","tonnage":35,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~35","equipment":{"$k":"~6","$v":["~7","~32",["clan-lrm-10","#2","turret",null],["ammo-lrm-standard","#3",null,"clan-lrm-10"],["ammo-lrm-standard","#4",null,"clan-lrm-10"],"~36","~37"]}},"1062":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"W-7 Facilities","primaryFactory":"Weingarten","communications":"Consolidated BMR 6c","targeting":"~28"},"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell","armor":"Forging OTR17b"},"text":{"overview":"~30","capabilities":"~31","deployment":"The ATM variant emerged from Clan Wolf production at Weingarten during the Jihad era, built on the framework of the Ammo variant. Its most significant change is the replacement of the LRM-10 with a turret-mounted ATM-6 launcher loaded with twenty rounds, providing access to standard, extended-range, and high-damage warhead types suited to different engagement ranges and target profiles. A fuel cell powerplant replaces the original internal combustion engine, pushing top speed to 80 km/h and improving tactical mobility over earlier variants. Clan Hell's Horses subsequently adopted the ATM variant after assuming control of the Weingarten production facility, integrating it into their garrison and second-line forces as a more capable successor to earlier Zorya designs.","battleHistory":"The Zorya emerged in 2842 from a recognition among Clan military planners that garrison operations required large numbers of affordable, easily crewed vehicles rather than small numbers of elite equipment. The name, drawn from ancient Slavic legend where the Zorya were dawn goddesses who guarded against cosmic destruction, was chosen to reflect the vehicle's protective garrison role rather than an aggressive frontline identity.\\n\\nProduction spread rapidly across multiple Homeworld facilities, and nearly every Clan integrated the Zorya into its second-line and garrison clusters. Clan Blood Spirit and Clan Cloud Cobra were among the earliest heavy adopters, fielding the vehicle in significant numbers through their lower-caste garrison forces. Blood Spirit Zoryas suffered severe losses during the Star Adder-Burrock Trial of Absorption, when Clan Blood Spirit intervened and found its garrison units badly outmatched. The losses proved difficult to replace quickly despite the vehicle's low unit cost.\\n\\nThe design gained renewed prominence following Operation Revival and the Refusal War. Clan Wolf, its warrior population significantly depleted by the Inner Sphere campaign and the fighting against Clan Jade Falcon, turned to the Zorya as an affordable platform for rebuilding garrison strength. Khan Vlad Ward authorized recruiting members of the artisan, laborer, and merchant castes through Trials of Position to crew Zoryas and other vehicles, acknowledging that conventional warrior recruitment could not fill the gap quickly enough.\\n\\nThe Zorya's longevity is a sign of sound, if unambitious, design. No single Clan has dominated its production, and the vehicle remains a fixture of Clan garrison forces well into the ilClan era."}},"name":"Zorya Light Tank","model":"(ATM)","tonnage":35,"tech":"clan","era":"jihad","engineType":"compact","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~35","equipment":{"$k":"~6","$v":["~7","~32",["atm-6","#2","turret",null],["ammo-clan-atm-standard","#3",null,"atm-6"],["ammo-clan-atm-standard","#4",null,"atm-6"],"~36","~37"]}}}`)};export{e as default};